1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,385
I built a small agent that refunds a customer and then emails

2
00:00:04,385 --> 00:00:07,375
them. I crashed it just before the email step,

3
00:00:07,375 --> 00:00:09,407
and restarted it from scratch.

4
00:00:09,407 --> 00:00:11,559
The customer was refunded twice.

5
00:00:11,559 --> 00:00:13,712
Resumed from a saved checkpoint,

6
00:00:13,712 --> 00:00:14,250
once.

7
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:17,396
That is one of three failures in this lesson.

8
00:00:17,396 --> 00:00:19,221
The second is a retry storm,

9
00:00:19,221 --> 00:00:22,816
where a simple fix cut the finish time from two hundred and

10
00:00:22,816 --> 00:00:24,300
eleven seconds to ten.

11
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:26,012
The third is a cost curve,

12
00:00:26,012 --> 00:00:29,778
where eight times the steps cost twenty-four times the tokens.

13
00:00:31,028 --> 00:00:34,468
Welcome to lesson nine of the cloud for A I engineers course,

14
00:00:34,468 --> 00:00:35,792
on The Code Notebook.

15
00:00:36,142 --> 00:00:39,276
The handwritten notes and the code for this lesson are linked in

16
00:00:39,276 --> 00:00:40,198
the description.

17
00:00:41,448 --> 00:00:43,189
Lesson eight built R A G,

18
00:00:43,189 --> 00:00:46,371
one of the two big patterns for A I applications.

19
00:00:46,371 --> 00:00:48,593
This lesson covers the other one,

20
00:00:48,593 --> 00:00:49,793
agents. That is,

21
00:00:49,793 --> 00:00:52,255
a model that decides what to do next,

22
00:00:52,255 --> 00:00:53,216
calls tools,

23
00:00:53,216 --> 00:00:55,317
and loops until a task is done.

24
00:00:55,667 --> 00:00:56,501
As always,

25
00:00:56,501 --> 00:00:58,109
I label my experiments.

26
00:00:58,109 --> 00:01:01,563
The agent loop uses a scripted stand in for the model,

27
00:01:01,563 --> 00:01:03,588
with no language model called.

28
00:01:03,588 --> 00:01:07,817
The retry and breaker results are simulations with assumed numbers.

29
00:01:07,817 --> 00:01:11,033
The token costs are arithmetic with assumed sizes.

30
00:01:12,283 --> 00:01:13,500
What is an agent?

31
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:16,225
A language model that uses tools in a loop,

32
00:01:16,225 --> 00:01:18,312
with memory and stop conditions.

33
00:01:18,312 --> 00:01:20,574
The classic recipe is called react.

34
00:01:20,574 --> 00:01:21,791
The model thinks,

35
00:01:21,791 --> 00:01:23,646
then acts by calling a tool,

36
00:01:23,646 --> 00:01:25,328
then observes the result,

37
00:01:25,328 --> 00:01:26,255
and repeats.

38
00:01:26,605 --> 00:01:27,937
A chat answers once.

39
00:01:27,937 --> 00:01:29,323
An agent keeps going.

40
00:01:29,323 --> 00:01:30,433
It might search,

41
00:01:30,433 --> 00:01:31,320
read a file,

42
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:32,319
call an A P I,

43
00:01:32,319 --> 00:01:33,483
check the result,

44
00:01:33,483 --> 00:01:34,482
and try again,

45
00:01:34,482 --> 00:01:37,699
until it decides it is finished or something stops it.

46
00:01:38,949 --> 00:01:40,888
Here is the loop in a few lines.

47
00:01:40,888 --> 00:01:42,342
The model picks a tool,

48
00:01:42,342 --> 00:01:43,526
your code runs it,

49
00:01:43,526 --> 00:01:45,250
and the result goes back in.

50
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:46,596
Then comes the check.

51
00:01:46,596 --> 00:01:47,781
Is the model done,

52
00:01:47,781 --> 00:01:49,289
or has a limit been hit?

53
00:01:49,639 --> 00:01:52,071
The model never runs anything itself.

54
00:01:52,071 --> 00:01:55,216
It writes a tool name and some J S O N arguments.

55
00:01:55,216 --> 00:01:56,877
Your code does the work,

56
00:01:56,877 --> 00:01:57,886
in a sandbox,

57
00:01:57,886 --> 00:01:59,013
with a timeout.

58
00:01:59,363 --> 00:02:01,759
And there are always two stop conditions.

59
00:02:01,759 --> 00:02:03,569
The model says it is finished,

60
00:02:03,569 --> 00:02:05,272
or the step budget runs out,

61
00:02:05,272 --> 00:02:07,082
in which case you fail loudly,

62
00:02:07,082 --> 00:02:07,933
not quietly.

63
00:02:09,183 --> 00:02:11,280
Tools matter more than prompts.

64
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:14,995
Describe each tool like documentation for a new colleague,

65
00:02:14,995 --> 00:02:16,972
with examples and edge cases.

66
00:02:16,972 --> 00:02:19,249
Return errors as ordinary results,

67
00:02:19,249 --> 00:02:22,424
so the model can read them and fix its next call.

68
00:02:22,774 --> 00:02:26,025
A trick from practice is to make mistakes impossible.

69
00:02:26,025 --> 00:02:29,732
One team found models kept getting relative file paths wrong,

70
00:02:29,732 --> 00:02:31,785
so they required absolute paths,

71
00:02:31,785 --> 00:02:34,238
and that whole class of error vanished.

72
00:02:35,488 --> 00:02:36,977
A useful distinction.

73
00:02:36,977 --> 00:02:39,838
A workflow follows a path you wrote in code.

74
00:02:39,838 --> 00:02:41,864
An agent decides its own path.

75
00:02:41,864 --> 00:02:44,843
Most useful systems should start as workflows,

76
00:02:44,843 --> 00:02:46,571
because they are cheaper,

77
00:02:46,571 --> 00:02:48,359
faster and easier to test.

78
00:02:48,709 --> 00:02:50,428
Common workflow patterns.

79
00:02:50,428 --> 00:02:52,384
Chain steps in a fixed order.

80
00:02:52,384 --> 00:02:54,932
Route a request to a specialist prompt.

81
00:02:54,932 --> 00:02:57,184
Run independent parts in parallel.

82
00:02:57,184 --> 00:03:00,089
Let an orchestrator split work among workers.

83
00:03:00,089 --> 00:03:02,104
Or loop a writer and a critic.

84
00:03:02,104 --> 00:03:05,660
Use a true agent only when you cannot predict the steps.

85
00:03:06,910 --> 00:03:09,448
What about many agents working together?

86
00:03:09,448 --> 00:03:11,582
A research system from Anthropic,

87
00:03:11,582 --> 00:03:14,063
with a lead agent and parallel helpers,

88
00:03:14,063 --> 00:03:17,582
beat a single agent by about ninety percent on its tests.

89
00:03:17,582 --> 00:03:19,428
But it used far more tokens.

90
00:03:19,778 --> 00:03:21,508
The rule of thumb is one,

91
00:03:21,508 --> 00:03:22,582
four, fifteen.

92
00:03:22,582 --> 00:03:24,730
A chat costs one unit of tokens.

93
00:03:24,730 --> 00:03:26,520
A single agent about four.

94
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:28,609
A team of agents about fifteen.

95
00:03:28,609 --> 00:03:30,279
Pay that only for broad,

96
00:03:30,279 --> 00:03:32,069
parallel, high value work,

97
00:03:32,069 --> 00:03:33,501
not for most coding,

98
00:03:33,501 --> 00:03:35,709
where steps depend on each other.

99
00:03:36,959 --> 00:03:38,330
Agents need memory,

100
00:03:38,330 --> 00:03:39,343
in two kinds.

101
00:03:39,343 --> 00:03:41,489
Short term is the thread so far,

102
00:03:41,489 --> 00:03:43,277
saved so a run can resume.

103
00:03:43,277 --> 00:03:46,019
Long term is what carries across sessions.

104
00:03:46,369 --> 00:03:48,765
Treat the context window as a budget.

105
00:03:48,765 --> 00:03:52,506
Every tool result you keep costs tokens on every later step.

106
00:03:52,506 --> 00:03:55,137
Summarise, drop what is no longer needed,

107
00:03:55,137 --> 00:03:58,995
and write long plans to storage instead of keeping them in the

108
00:03:58,995 --> 00:03:59,638
prompt.

109
00:04:00,888 --> 00:04:02,284
Now the cost curve.

110
00:04:02,284 --> 00:04:05,258
A language model has no memory between calls,

111
00:04:05,258 --> 00:04:08,718
so an agent re sends its whole history at every step.

112
00:04:08,718 --> 00:04:12,056
Step ten carries everything from steps one to nine.

113
00:04:12,406 --> 00:04:14,724
I worked it out with assumed sizes.

114
00:04:14,724 --> 00:04:17,994
A system prompt with tools of four thousand tokens.

115
00:04:17,994 --> 00:04:21,976
Each step adds a hundred tokens of model reply and five hundred

116
00:04:21,976 --> 00:04:23,105
of tool result.

117
00:04:23,105 --> 00:04:24,473
That is arithmetic,

118
00:04:24,473 --> 00:04:25,780
not a measurement.

119
00:04:27,030 --> 00:04:30,190
Five steps bill twenty-six thousand input tokens.

120
00:04:30,190 --> 00:04:31,024
Ten steps,

121
00:04:31,024 --> 00:04:32,515
sixty-seven thousand,

122
00:04:32,515 --> 00:04:34,422
two point six times as much.

123
00:04:34,422 --> 00:04:35,436
Twenty steps,

124
00:04:35,436 --> 00:04:37,760
a hundred and ninety-four thousand,

125
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:39,370
seven and a half times.

126
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:40,625
Forty steps,

127
00:04:40,625 --> 00:04:43,396
six hundred and twenty-eight thousand tokens.

128
00:04:43,396 --> 00:04:44,867
Eight times the steps,

129
00:04:44,867 --> 00:04:46,620
twenty-four times the cost.

130
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:49,901
The cost grows with the square of the number of steps,

131
00:04:49,901 --> 00:04:51,994
so long loops get expensive fast.

132
00:04:53,244 --> 00:04:55,141
The rescue is prompt caching.

133
00:04:55,141 --> 00:04:58,705
The provider keeps the work for an identical prompt start,

134
00:04:58,705 --> 00:05:01,867
and charges only about a tenth for reading it back.

135
00:05:01,867 --> 00:05:04,224
I used the notebook's example prices.

136
00:05:04,224 --> 00:05:07,214
Two dollars per million tokens to read normally,

137
00:05:07,214 --> 00:05:09,398
two fifty to write into the cache,

138
00:05:09,398 --> 00:05:11,123
ten cents to read from it.

139
00:05:11,473 --> 00:05:16,042
The twenty step agent costs about thirty-nine cents without caching and about

140
00:05:16,042 --> 00:05:17,283
six cents with it,

141
00:05:17,283 --> 00:05:18,918
eighty-five percent less.

142
00:05:18,918 --> 00:05:20,667
And the notebook's example,

143
00:05:20,667 --> 00:05:23,939
a hundred calls sharing a fifty thousand token prefix,

144
00:05:23,939 --> 00:05:26,533
falls from ten dollars to sixty-two cents,

145
00:05:26,533 --> 00:05:27,887
ninety-four percent.

146
00:05:29,137 --> 00:05:30,473
Caching has rules.

147
00:05:30,473 --> 00:05:32,233
Put stable content first,

148
00:05:32,233 --> 00:05:34,054
and volatile content last.

149
00:05:34,054 --> 00:05:37,211
Never put a timestamp or a user name at the top,

150
00:05:37,211 --> 00:05:40,246
because one changed character ruins the match.

151
00:05:40,246 --> 00:05:42,614
The order of invalidation is tools,

152
00:05:42,614 --> 00:05:44,010
then system prompt,

153
00:05:44,010 --> 00:05:45,103
then messages,

154
00:05:45,103 --> 00:05:48,563
so changing a tool definition throws away everything.

155
00:05:48,913 --> 00:05:52,017
Prompts below a minimum length are silently not cached.

156
00:05:52,017 --> 00:05:53,858
And caches belong to one model,

157
00:05:53,858 --> 00:05:56,909
so if you fall back to a different model in an outage,

158
00:05:56,909 --> 00:05:58,119
you lose the cache.

159
00:05:59,369 --> 00:06:01,689
A different idea is semantic caching.

160
00:06:01,689 --> 00:06:02,990
Embed the question,

161
00:06:02,990 --> 00:06:06,102
and if a very similar question was answered before,

162
00:06:06,102 --> 00:06:09,044
return the old answer without calling the model.

163
00:06:09,044 --> 00:06:09,949
It is cheap,

164
00:06:09,949 --> 00:06:11,081
but it is lossy.

165
00:06:11,431 --> 00:06:14,903
Cancel my order and cancel my subscription look similar,

166
00:06:14,903 --> 00:06:16,697
but need different answers.

167
00:06:16,697 --> 00:06:20,805
And answers built from one user's documents must never be served to

168
00:06:20,805 --> 00:06:23,467
another. Use it only for frequently asked,

169
00:06:23,467 --> 00:06:24,740
generic questions,

170
00:06:24,740 --> 00:06:26,303
with strict thresholds,

171
00:06:26,303 --> 00:06:28,733
short lifetimes and keys per customer.

172
00:06:29,983 --> 00:06:31,174
Now protocols.

173
00:06:31,174 --> 00:06:31,836
M C P,

174
00:06:31,836 --> 00:06:33,888
the Model Context Protocol,

175
00:06:33,888 --> 00:06:37,065
is a standard way for an agent to use tools.

176
00:06:37,065 --> 00:06:39,250
Your application is the host.

177
00:06:39,250 --> 00:06:41,500
It runs one client per server.

178
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:43,420
Each server offers tools,

179
00:06:43,420 --> 00:06:45,140
resources and prompts,

180
00:06:45,140 --> 00:06:46,663
over J S O N R P C.

181
00:06:47,013 --> 00:06:49,111
Think of it like U S B for tools.

182
00:06:49,111 --> 00:06:50,755
Write a tool server once,

183
00:06:50,755 --> 00:06:53,023
and any compatible agent can use it.

184
00:06:53,023 --> 00:06:54,384
Two ways to connect,

185
00:06:54,384 --> 00:06:55,518
a local process,

186
00:06:55,518 --> 00:06:58,976
or a web connection that can be shared and secured with O

187
00:06:58,976 --> 00:06:59,486
Auth.

188
00:06:59,836 --> 00:07:02,192
One way to remember who controls what.

189
00:07:02,192 --> 00:07:04,322
Tools are controlled by the model.

190
00:07:04,322 --> 00:07:05,724
Resources by the app.

191
00:07:05,724 --> 00:07:07,182
Prompts by the person.

192
00:07:08,432 --> 00:07:11,596
A big change landed in July twenty twenty-six.

193
00:07:11,596 --> 00:07:14,064
The new M C P version is stateless.

194
00:07:14,064 --> 00:07:17,418
There is no opening handshake and no session I D.

195
00:07:17,418 --> 00:07:20,076
Every request carries its own version,

196
00:07:20,076 --> 00:07:21,974
identity and capabilities.

197
00:07:22,324 --> 00:07:26,123
That means any copy of a server behind a load balancer can

198
00:07:26,123 --> 00:07:27,532
answer any request.

199
00:07:27,532 --> 00:07:30,657
You can run an M C P server as a plain function

200
00:07:30,657 --> 00:07:32,311
in Lambda or Cloud Run.

201
00:07:32,311 --> 00:07:36,845
New headers also let gateways route and limit per tool without opening

202
00:07:36,845 --> 00:07:38,131
the message body.

203
00:07:39,381 --> 00:07:40,699
A second protocol,

204
00:07:40,699 --> 00:07:41,418
A two A,

205
00:07:41,418 --> 00:07:42,975
is for agent to agent.

206
00:07:42,975 --> 00:07:46,809
When one agent wants to hand a task to another team's agent,

207
00:07:46,809 --> 00:07:50,223
it finds it through a public card listing its skills,

208
00:07:50,223 --> 00:07:51,301
and delegates.

209
00:07:51,301 --> 00:07:55,195
It reached version one point zero in March twenty twenty-six.

210
00:07:55,545 --> 00:07:56,749
Short version.

211
00:07:56,749 --> 00:07:59,156
M C P is an agent calling tools.

212
00:07:59,156 --> 00:08:02,634
A two A is an agent delegating to another agent.

213
00:08:02,634 --> 00:08:04,640
Most teams need M C P now,

214
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:07,984
and A two A when agents cross team boundaries.

215
00:08:09,234 --> 00:08:11,312
A chat turn fits one web request.

216
00:08:11,312 --> 00:08:12,322
An agent task,

217
00:08:12,322 --> 00:08:13,950
lasting minutes or hours,

218
00:08:13,950 --> 00:08:15,691
with approvals and crashes,

219
00:08:15,691 --> 00:08:16,421
does not.

220
00:08:16,421 --> 00:08:18,666
So make it asynchronous and durable.

221
00:08:19,016 --> 00:08:21,528
The A P I returns a task I D at once.

222
00:08:21,528 --> 00:08:23,305
The task goes on a queue.

223
00:08:23,305 --> 00:08:26,000
A durable workflow engine runs the loop,

224
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,125
and every model or tool call is a retried step.

225
00:08:29,125 --> 00:08:30,718
Progress streams back,

226
00:08:30,718 --> 00:08:32,372
code runs in a sandbox,

227
00:08:32,372 --> 00:08:35,067
and state is checkpointed to a database.

228
00:08:36,317 --> 00:08:38,594
Let me prove why checkpoints matter.

229
00:08:38,594 --> 00:08:40,473
I built a working agent loop.

230
00:08:40,473 --> 00:08:41,497
A step budget,

231
00:08:41,497 --> 00:08:43,660
tool errors returned to the model,

232
00:08:43,660 --> 00:08:44,856
an approval gate,

233
00:08:44,856 --> 00:08:47,360
and a checkpoint saved after every step.

234
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:49,466
The model is a scripted stand in,

235
00:08:49,466 --> 00:08:51,060
so nothing is generated.

236
00:08:51,410 --> 00:08:52,159
The task.

237
00:08:52,159 --> 00:08:53,370
Look up an order,

238
00:08:53,370 --> 00:08:54,523
read the policy,

239
00:08:54,523 --> 00:08:55,791
check eligibility,

240
00:08:55,791 --> 00:08:56,828
draft a reply,

241
00:08:56,828 --> 00:08:57,808
get approval,

242
00:08:57,808 --> 00:08:58,903
issue a refund,

243
00:08:58,903 --> 00:08:59,941
send an email,

244
00:08:59,941 --> 00:09:01,209
update the ticket.

245
00:09:01,209 --> 00:09:02,362
Nine tool steps.

246
00:09:02,362 --> 00:09:05,243
I kill the process just before the email step,

247
00:09:05,243 --> 00:09:06,742
then recover two ways.

248
00:09:07,992 --> 00:09:09,519
Restart from step one,

249
00:09:09,519 --> 00:09:10,870
with no protection.

250
00:09:10,870 --> 00:09:12,809
Fourteen tool calls in total,

251
00:09:12,809 --> 00:09:15,159
and the customer was refunded twice.

252
00:09:15,159 --> 00:09:17,626
With an idempotency key on the refund,

253
00:09:17,626 --> 00:09:19,094
still fourteen calls,

254
00:09:19,094 --> 00:09:20,210
but one refund.

255
00:09:20,560 --> 00:09:22,266
Resume from the checkpoint.

256
00:09:22,266 --> 00:09:24,191
Only eight tool calls in total,

257
00:09:24,191 --> 00:09:25,237
and one refund.

258
00:09:25,237 --> 00:09:26,722
Resume, do not restart.

259
00:09:27,972 --> 00:09:29,087
So four rules.

260
00:09:29,087 --> 00:09:31,068
Checkpoint after every step,

261
00:09:31,068 --> 00:09:34,784
so a crash at step thirty-seven resumes at thirty-seven.

262
00:09:34,784 --> 00:09:38,499
Put an idempotency key on every tool with a side effect,

263
00:09:38,499 --> 00:09:41,286
so a replay cannot charge or email twice.

264
00:09:41,636 --> 00:09:44,127
Treat human approvals as a durable pause,

265
00:09:44,127 --> 00:09:45,567
not a blocked process,

266
00:09:45,567 --> 00:09:47,616
because approvals can take hours.

267
00:09:47,616 --> 00:09:49,720
And when you deploy a new version,

268
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:53,153
let running tasks finish on the version they started with.

269
00:09:54,403 --> 00:09:55,608
Agents run code,

270
00:09:55,608 --> 00:09:57,355
so run it somewhere safe.

271
00:09:57,355 --> 00:09:58,380
Three levels.

272
00:09:58,380 --> 00:10:01,935
A micro virtual machine gives each task its own kernel,

273
00:10:01,935 --> 00:10:03,442
and is the strongest.

274
00:10:03,442 --> 00:10:05,611
G Visor intercepts system calls,

275
00:10:05,611 --> 00:10:06,816
a middle option,

276
00:10:06,816 --> 00:10:08,323
often used with GPUs.

277
00:10:08,323 --> 00:10:11,155
A plain container shares the host's kernel,

278
00:10:11,155 --> 00:10:13,023
so only for code you trust.

279
00:10:13,373 --> 00:10:14,834
For untrusted code,

280
00:10:14,834 --> 00:10:16,104
use a micro V M.

281
00:10:16,104 --> 00:10:18,263
Add limits on time and memory,

282
00:10:18,263 --> 00:10:20,613
control outbound network traffic,

283
00:10:20,613 --> 00:10:23,471
and never put secrets inside the sandbox.

284
00:10:24,721 --> 00:10:27,238
Failures also come from the outside.

285
00:10:27,238 --> 00:10:28,245
Rate limits,

286
00:10:28,245 --> 00:10:29,314
busy servers,

287
00:10:29,314 --> 00:10:32,083
outages. Reliability is built in layers.

288
00:10:32,083 --> 00:10:34,223
The S D K retries with jitter.

289
00:10:34,223 --> 00:10:36,488
A gateway adds circuit breakers,

290
00:10:36,488 --> 00:10:38,061
fallbacks and quotas.

291
00:10:38,061 --> 00:10:40,578
The workflow engine retries durably.

292
00:10:40,578 --> 00:10:44,228
And the app streams results and uses idempotency keys.

293
00:10:44,578 --> 00:10:46,849
Next I will measure two of those layers.

294
00:10:46,849 --> 00:10:48,397
First, why jitter matters.

295
00:10:48,397 --> 00:10:50,358
Then what a circuit breaker saves.

296
00:10:51,608 --> 00:10:53,998
When a server says too many requests,

297
00:10:53,998 --> 00:10:55,630
with a four twenty-nine,

298
00:10:55,630 --> 00:10:56,679
clients retry.

299
00:10:56,679 --> 00:10:58,836
How they wait matters enormously.

300
00:10:58,836 --> 00:11:02,509
I simulated two hundred clients all calling at second zero,

301
00:11:02,509 --> 00:11:05,715
to a server that accepts fifty requests per second.

302
00:11:05,715 --> 00:11:07,755
That capacity is my assumption.

303
00:11:08,105 --> 00:11:09,074
Three rules.

304
00:11:09,074 --> 00:11:10,164
Retry at once,

305
00:11:10,164 --> 00:11:11,860
every tenth of a second.

306
00:11:11,860 --> 00:11:14,464
Exponential backoff with no randomness.

307
00:11:14,464 --> 00:11:17,190
And exponential backoff with full jitter,

308
00:11:17,190 --> 00:11:19,794
a random wait between zero and the cap.

309
00:11:19,794 --> 00:11:22,458
Each client gives up after twelve tries.

310
00:11:23,708 --> 00:11:27,404
Retrying at once made two thousand and ten rejected attempts,

311
00:11:27,404 --> 00:11:30,531
and a hundred and forty clients gave up completely.

312
00:11:30,531 --> 00:11:34,681
Backoff without jitter made the same two thousand and ten rejections,

313
00:11:34,681 --> 00:11:38,491
and the last client finished at two hundred and eleven seconds.

314
00:11:38,841 --> 00:11:43,018
Full jitter made only four hundred and forty-four rejected attempts,

315
00:11:43,018 --> 00:11:45,338
finished in nine point nine seconds,

316
00:11:45,338 --> 00:11:46,672
and nobody gave up.

317
00:11:47,922 --> 00:11:49,384
Here is the other view.

318
00:11:49,384 --> 00:11:50,412
Without jitter,

319
00:11:50,412 --> 00:11:54,147
all the failed clients wait the same time and come back together,

320
00:11:54,147 --> 00:11:54,850
in waves.

321
00:11:54,850 --> 00:11:56,637
Each wave hits the same wall.

322
00:11:56,987 --> 00:11:59,369
With full jitter the retries spread out,

323
00:11:59,369 --> 00:12:01,805
so the server serves them as it has room.

324
00:12:01,805 --> 00:12:02,671
Same server,

325
00:12:02,671 --> 00:12:03,591
same clients.

326
00:12:03,591 --> 00:12:05,757
The only change was a random number.

327
00:12:07,007 --> 00:12:08,408
The practical rules.

328
00:12:08,408 --> 00:12:11,909
Wait a random time between zero and the smaller of a cap

329
00:12:11,909 --> 00:12:14,536
and base times two to the attempt number.

330
00:12:14,536 --> 00:12:17,162
If the server sends a retry after header,

331
00:12:17,162 --> 00:12:18,213
obey it first.

332
00:12:18,563 --> 00:12:20,807
Retry only what can succeed later.

333
00:12:20,807 --> 00:12:22,047
Four twenty-nine,

334
00:12:22,047 --> 00:12:23,051
five hundred,

335
00:12:23,051 --> 00:12:25,354
five oh three and five twenty-nine.

336
00:12:25,354 --> 00:12:27,657
Never blindly retry a four hundred,

337
00:12:27,657 --> 00:12:30,019
because the request itself is wrong.

338
00:12:31,269 --> 00:12:34,481
A circuit breaker protects you when a backend is down.

339
00:12:34,481 --> 00:12:35,810
It has three states.

340
00:12:35,810 --> 00:12:36,972
Closed is normal,

341
00:12:36,972 --> 00:12:38,467
and it counts failures.

342
00:12:38,467 --> 00:12:40,405
After enough failures it opens,

343
00:12:40,405 --> 00:12:41,900
and requests fail fast,

344
00:12:41,900 --> 00:12:43,229
or go to a fallback,

345
00:12:43,229 --> 00:12:45,444
without touching the broken service.

346
00:12:45,794 --> 00:12:47,853
After a cooldown it goes half open,

347
00:12:47,853 --> 00:12:49,807
and lets a trial request through.

348
00:12:49,807 --> 00:12:50,758
If that works,

349
00:12:50,758 --> 00:12:51,497
it closes.

350
00:12:51,497 --> 00:12:52,078
If not,

351
00:12:52,078 --> 00:12:53,082
it opens again.

352
00:12:53,082 --> 00:12:54,666
Like a fuse in your house,

353
00:12:54,666 --> 00:12:56,303
but one that resets itself.

354
00:12:57,553 --> 00:13:00,782
I simulated a backend that is down for thirty seconds,

355
00:13:00,782 --> 00:13:04,011
with twenty requests per second arriving for a minute.

356
00:13:04,011 --> 00:13:05,236
Without a breaker,

357
00:13:05,236 --> 00:13:07,630
six hundred calls hit the dead backend,

358
00:13:07,630 --> 00:13:09,245
each waiting and failing.

359
00:13:09,595 --> 00:13:13,493
With a breaker that opens after five failures and waits ten seconds

360
00:13:13,493 --> 00:13:14,537
before a trial,

361
00:13:14,537 --> 00:13:15,800
seven calls hit it.

362
00:13:15,800 --> 00:13:16,678
Same outage,

363
00:13:16,678 --> 00:13:18,546
a fraction of the wasted work,

364
00:13:18,546 --> 00:13:20,413
and quick fallbacks for users.

365
00:13:21,663 --> 00:13:23,382
What should the fallback be?

366
00:13:23,382 --> 00:13:25,747
First, the same model in another region.

367
00:13:25,747 --> 00:13:27,467
Only then a different model.

368
00:13:27,467 --> 00:13:29,616
A different model changes behaviour,

369
00:13:29,616 --> 00:13:31,766
because prompts are tuned per model,

370
00:13:31,766 --> 00:13:33,593
and it loses the prompt cache.

371
00:13:33,943 --> 00:13:37,816
An A I gateway sits between your apps and the model providers.

372
00:13:37,816 --> 00:13:39,459
It offers one interface,

373
00:13:39,459 --> 00:13:40,398
key storage,

374
00:13:40,398 --> 00:13:41,572
quotas per team,

375
00:13:41,572 --> 00:13:43,626
routing, fallbacks and metrics.

376
00:13:43,626 --> 00:13:45,387
Open source options exist,

377
00:13:45,387 --> 00:13:48,791
and the simple rule is to extend what you already run.

378
00:13:50,041 --> 00:13:52,952
Two small features that improve reliability.

379
00:13:52,952 --> 00:13:55,621
Streaming shows the first words quickly,

380
00:13:55,621 --> 00:13:58,351
and avoids idle timeouts on long answers.

381
00:13:58,351 --> 00:14:02,355
And structured outputs make the model follow a schema exactly.

382
00:14:02,705 --> 00:14:03,542
A caution.

383
00:14:03,542 --> 00:14:05,456
Valid does not mean correct.

384
00:14:05,456 --> 00:14:08,685
A response can be perfect J S O N and still wrong.

385
00:14:08,685 --> 00:14:10,000
Check the content,

386
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:11,376
not just the shape.

387
00:14:12,626 --> 00:14:13,778
Put it together.

388
00:14:13,778 --> 00:14:16,197
The same layers appear on every cloud.

389
00:14:16,197 --> 00:14:18,502
An edge firewall and A P I in front,

390
00:14:18,502 --> 00:14:20,518
then your app or agent runtime,

391
00:14:20,518 --> 00:14:22,822
then an A I gateway with guardrails,

392
00:14:22,822 --> 00:14:23,975
then the models.

393
00:14:23,975 --> 00:14:25,703
Beside them sit retrieval,

394
00:14:25,703 --> 00:14:28,122
state, queues with a durable workflow,

395
00:14:28,122 --> 00:14:29,678
sandboxed tool servers,

396
00:14:29,678 --> 00:14:31,118
identity and tracing.

397
00:14:31,468 --> 00:14:32,785
The names differ.

398
00:14:32,785 --> 00:14:35,042
A W S has Bedrock and AgentCore,

399
00:14:35,042 --> 00:14:37,738
Google has Gemini and its Agent Engine,

400
00:14:37,738 --> 00:14:39,118
Azure has Foundry.

401
00:14:39,118 --> 00:14:40,685
This is my synthesis,

402
00:14:40,685 --> 00:14:42,128
not a vendor table.

403
00:14:42,128 --> 00:14:44,259
The shape is what to remember.

404
00:14:45,509 --> 00:14:47,044
For ShopNest Copilot.

405
00:14:47,044 --> 00:14:48,517
Start as a workflow,

406
00:14:48,517 --> 00:14:49,561
not an agent.

407
00:14:49,561 --> 00:14:53,429
A fixed route from ticket to intent to retrieval to answer.

408
00:14:53,429 --> 00:14:56,559
Add a refund tool only behind a human approval,

409
00:14:56,559 --> 00:14:59,322
with an idempotency key and a checkpoint.

410
00:14:59,672 --> 00:15:01,628
Tools are served over M C P,

411
00:15:01,628 --> 00:15:04,624
stable prompt content goes first for caching,

412
00:15:04,624 --> 00:15:08,964
and calls go through a gateway with jittered retries and a breaker.

413
00:15:08,964 --> 00:15:13,121
We add loops and more agents only if the evaluations from lesson

414
00:15:13,121 --> 00:15:14,588
seven show they pay.

415
00:15:15,838 --> 00:15:17,808
Four words to carry forward.

416
00:15:17,808 --> 00:15:19,040
Idempotency key:

417
00:15:19,040 --> 00:15:22,610
a label that makes a repeated action happen only once.

418
00:15:22,610 --> 00:15:25,442
Checkpoint: saved progress to resume from.

419
00:15:25,792 --> 00:15:27,976
Jitter: a random delay in retries.

420
00:15:27,976 --> 00:15:29,355
And circuit breaker:

421
00:15:29,355 --> 00:15:32,228
a switch that stops calls to a broken service.

422
00:15:33,478 --> 00:15:34,754
Interview corner.

423
00:15:34,754 --> 00:15:38,278
Asked how you make a long running agent safe to retry,

424
00:15:38,278 --> 00:15:40,526
say: checkpoint after every step,

425
00:15:40,526 --> 00:15:42,409
resume rather than restart,

426
00:15:42,409 --> 00:15:45,811
and put idempotency keys on tools with side effects.

427
00:15:45,811 --> 00:15:46,723
In my test,

428
00:15:46,723 --> 00:15:49,578
a restart refunded twice and a resume once.

429
00:15:49,928 --> 00:15:51,892
Asked why add jitter to retries,

430
00:15:51,892 --> 00:15:52,983
say: without it,

431
00:15:52,983 --> 00:15:54,456
clients retry in waves.

432
00:15:54,456 --> 00:15:56,038
With two hundred clients,

433
00:15:56,038 --> 00:15:59,911
full jitter finished in ten seconds against two hundred and eleven,

434
00:15:59,911 --> 00:16:02,093
with a third of the wasted attempts.

435
00:16:03,343 --> 00:16:04,139
Your turn.

436
00:16:04,139 --> 00:16:06,753
The zip for this lesson has three scripts.

437
00:16:06,753 --> 00:16:08,173
The crash experiment,

438
00:16:08,173 --> 00:16:10,219
the retry and breaker simulator,

439
00:16:10,219 --> 00:16:12,151
and the agent cost calculator.

440
00:16:12,151 --> 00:16:15,845
Remove the idempotency key and watch the refund happen twice.

441
00:16:16,195 --> 00:16:19,981
Then change the capacity or the number of clients in the retry

442
00:16:19,981 --> 00:16:23,767
test. One changed number teaches more than an hour of reading.

443
00:16:25,017 --> 00:16:28,040
Recap. An agent is a model with tools in a loop,

444
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:29,726
so start with a workflow.

445
00:16:29,726 --> 00:16:31,703
The loop re sends its history,

446
00:16:31,703 --> 00:16:35,365
so eight times the steps cost twenty-four times the tokens,

447
00:16:35,365 --> 00:16:39,318
and caching cuts it by about eighty-five to ninety-four percent.

448
00:16:39,668 --> 00:16:42,644
Checkpoint, resume and use idempotency keys,

449
00:16:42,644 --> 00:16:44,442
or a crash refunds twice.

450
00:16:44,442 --> 00:16:48,038
Jitter turned two hundred and eleven seconds into ten,

451
00:16:48,038 --> 00:16:51,510
and a breaker cut six hundred wasted calls to seven.

452
00:16:51,510 --> 00:16:52,502
Next lesson:

453
00:16:52,502 --> 00:16:54,237
security and governance.

454
00:16:55,487 --> 00:16:56,766
In the next lesson:

455
00:16:56,766 --> 00:16:58,323
security and governance.

456
00:16:58,323 --> 00:17:01,158
Identity, prompt injection and least privilege.

457
00:17:01,508 --> 00:17:03,101
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

458
00:17:03,101 --> 00:17:04,375
See you in the notebook.

